  "answer_markdown": "## The page problem is clarity and trust at the buying moment—not a reason to cut price yet\n\nYour store funnel shows **112 add-to-cart sessions from 4,452 sessions (2.5%)**, while **101 reached checkout and 34 completed checkout (33.7% of checkout reachers)**. That points first to a weak/unclear product-page decision, then to a meaningful checkout drop. It does **not** prove the page alone is the issue: Meta traffic quality varied sharply by campaign, and Meta/Shopify totals are not one sequential funnel.\n\n### Fix these first (before sending more paid traffic)\n\n1. **Remove the stock contradiction immediately.**  \n   The public-page memo found both “Out of stock” and “Only 48 kits left. Delivery in 2–7 days.” If a parent sees that conflict, they will not risk payment. Use one inventory source for stock state, CTA state, delivery promise and any scarcity. Remove “48 left” unless it is live and accurate.\n\n2. **Consolidate the two product URLs.**  \n   Your Shopify screenshots show 2,875 sessions on `/products/all-in-one-childproofing-kit-for-6-danger-areas-set-up-in-30-minutes` and 1,498 on `/products/the-peach-bucket-6-in-1-child-safety-kit`. That means roughly **66% of recorded sessions landed on the former URL**. Pick one canonical PDP, 301-redirect the other, and make sure ads, pixel events, cart and product content all point to the same version. Otherwise improvements may reach only a minority of visitors.\n\n3. **Rebuild the first screen around “Will this work in my home?”**  \n   The existing concrete strengths—Indian socket mix, no-drill setup, extra adhesive and setup support—should appear beside price and Add to Cart, before broad reassurance language.\n\n### Replacement purchase-box copy\n\n**Headline:**  \n**Childproof 6 common danger zones—without drilling into your home**\n\n**Subhead:**  \nOne kit for sharp edges, accessible sockets, cabinets, fridge doors and interior doors. Designed around common Indian homes.\n\n**What’s in this ₹3,299 kit**  \n- 8 m edge protection + 12 corner guards  \n- 12 socket protectors: **10 × 5A and 2 × 15A**  \n- 10 cabinet locks + 2 fridge locks + 4 door guards  \n- Extra adhesive and 1:1 installation video support\n\n**Before you buy**  \nBest suited to common Indian modular switchboards, wooden furniture/doors, modular kitchens and refrigerators. Please check your socket and surface type before ordering.  \n\n**This kit helps cover common contact and access hazards. It does not replace supervision, furniture anchoring, stair gates, or safe storage of medicines and chemicals.**\n\n**CTA:** `Check fit & add to cart`  \nUnder it: `Review your cart before payment • Delivery timing shown at checkout`  \nOnly use that line if it precisely reflects your checkout flow.\n\n### Add four short, expandable blocks directly below the CTA\n\n1. **“Will it fit my home?”**  \n   Show photos/line drawings of compatible 5A/15A socket styles, cabinet types and surfaces. Add a visible support route: **“Unsure? Send us a photo of your socket/cabinet before ordering.”** Do not claim compatibility with every surface.\n\n2. **“See each item in use”**  \n   A six-tile visual: edge, corner, socket, cabinet, fridge, door. Each tile needs one sentence explaining the scenario it addresses and one real installation image/video. This makes ₹3,299 feel like a defined system rather than a bag of parts.\n\n3. **“Installation in your actual home”**  \n   Show a 30–45 second uncut setup clip: clean surface → apply → finished result. State the support mechanics precisely: when buyers receive the video, how they ask questions, and support hours/response expectation only if you can meet it.\n\n4. **“Returns, replacement and delivery—plain English”**  \n   Put the real conditions next to the promise: 30-day return eligibility, refund timeline, 720-day replacement eligibility/failure definition, claim process, exclusions, dispatch timing and delivery coverage. Keep the existing guarantees only after these terms are verified and linked.\n\n### Make the bundle objection visible\n\nSome parents will want only socket guards or cabinet locks. Do not force them to hunt for the build-your-own option.\n\nAdd this directly under the purchase box:\n\n> **Not sure you need all 6 categories?**  \n> Start with the full-home kit above, or **build a smaller kit for your home** →\n\nThis is not an argument that the full kit is overpriced; it is a lower-commitment path for undecided visitors. Show the exact contents and any genuine saving versus individual items—never imply a saving you have not calculated.\n\n### Checkout checks (second priority)\n\nThe 34/101 checkout-completion gap is worth auditing after the page fixes. On mobile, test a full purchase yourself for: guest checkout, UPI and other relevant payment methods, unexpected shipping/tax/fees, address friction, payment failures, and whether delivery timing is visible before payment. Do not advertise free delivery, COD, or payment methods unless they are actually available.\n\n### What I would not do now\n\n- Do not use fake scarcity or the currently conflicting stock message.\n- Do not add unverified safety tests, certifications, injury-prevention promises or endorsements.\n- Do not lead with “Top 1% of Indian Parents.” Test removing it; “made for common Indian homes” is more concrete and less exclusionary.\n- Do not discount first. At the listed ₹3,299, product + stated shipping + 2% payment fee are about **₹1,066 before ads, packaging, taxes and RTO costs**. A price cut can make an already tight acquisition equation worse.\n\n### 7-day implementation order\n1. Inventory/CTA/delivery audit; canonical redirect.  \n2. Replace above-the-fold purchase block on the canonical page.  \n3. Add compatibility, exclusions and policy-detail accordions.  \n4. Add real installation visuals and a smaller-kit route.  \n5. Run mobile test orders and review payment/shipping friction.  \n6. Track canonical-PDP view → ATC, ATC → checkout, checkout → purchase separately by device and campaign. Do not judge the edit on CTA clicks alone.",
